{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And Joseph's brothers saw that their father was dead.</b> What is וַיִּרְאוּ? They took notice at his death regarding Joseph — for they had been accustomed to dine at Joseph's table, and he would bring them near for their father's honor, and from the time Jacob died he no longer brought them near (Bereshit Rabbah)." "<b>לו ישטמנו.</b> Perhaps he will hate us. לוּ is divided into many senses: there is לוּ that serves in the language of request and the language of condition, such as ״לוּ יְהִי כִדְבָרֶךָ,״ ״לוּ שְׁמָעֵנִי,״ ״לוּ הוֹאַלְנוּ,״ ״לוּ מַתְנוּ״; and there is לוּ that serves in the language of 'if' and 'perhaps,' such as ״לוּ חָכְמוּ״ (Deuteronomy 32), ״לוּ הִקְשַׁבְתָּ לְמִצְוֹתַי״ (Isaiah 48), and ״לוּ אָנֹכִי שֹׁקֵל עַל כַּפַּי״ (II Samuel 18); and there is לוּ that serves in the language of 'perhaps' — ״לוּ יִשְׂטְמֵנוּ״ — and it has no parallel in Scripture, and it is in the sense of אוּלַי, like ״אוּלַי לֹא תֵלֵךְ הָאִשָּׁה אַחֲרֶיךָ״ — it is in the sense of 'perhaps'; and there is אוּלַי in the language of request, such as ״אוּלַי יֵרָאֶה ד׳ בְּעֵינָיו״ (II Samuel 16), ״אוּלַי ד׳ אוֹתִי״ (Joshua 14) — these are like ״לוּ יְהִי כִדְבָרֶךָ״; and there is אוּלַי in the sense of 'if' — ״אוּלַי יֵשׁ נ׳ צַדִּיקִם.״"], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 50, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:04:31.962017393Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}